---
title: French omelette
description: Lesson 4.1. How egg sets, and stopping before it has finished.
servings: 1
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, eggs-and-emulsions]
lesson: "4.1"
technique: setting eggs
status: draft
---

> Technique: setting eggs gently. Egg turns from liquid to solid over a
> narrow band of temperature, and a pan is far hotter than that band.
> The skill is to keep the egg moving so it sets in small soft curds,
> and to take it off the heat before it has finished.

Put @oil{1/2%tsp} in a #small non-stick frying pan{}(about 20 cm, with a lid) and set it over low heat for ~{10%minutes}.

> Why so long: a slow preheat evens out the pan's hot spots, so the
> omelette cooks evenly and does not come out blotchy.
-- source: "Perfect French Omelets", America's Test Kitchen, 2013-04-11, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/4594-perfect-french-omelets

Meanwhile, beat @eggs{3} with @salt{1%pinch} in a #bowl{} until no streaks of white remain. Stir in @cold butter{15%g}(cut into small dice).

> Why butter in the eggs: fat coats the egg proteins and stops them
> bonding tightly to each other, which keeps the curds soft and creamy.
-- source: Andrea Geary, "Creamy French Scrambled Eggs", America's Test Kitchen, 2017-12-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/578-creamy-french-scrambled-eggs

Turn the heat up to medium-high and pour in the eggs. Stir all the time in small quick circles with a #wooden fork{} for about ~{1%minute}, until the egg is mostly small soft curds with a little liquid egg still between them.

Take the pan off the heat. Smooth the egg into an even layer, cover the pan, and leave it for ~{1%minute}.

> Why stir, and why stop early: constant stirring keeps the curds small
> and the texture even. The pan and the egg hold enough heat to finish
> the job off the hob, without toughening the egg.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Make Omelets Like a Pro", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-08-03, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3516-make-omelets-like-a-pro ; "Perfect French Omelets" (as above)

> Science: egg white is a mixture of proteins that set at different
> temperatures. Ovotransferrin, 12% of the white's protein, unfolds at
> about 61 to 65 °C; ovalbumin, 54% of it, at about 75 to 84 °C. As a
> protein unfolds it bonds to its neighbours and the liquid becomes a
> gel, and for ovalbumin that does not reverse on cooling. Egg white
> starts to lose its fluidity at around 60 °C, so the whole change
> happens between roughly 60 and 85 °C. Look up: protein denaturation,
> coagulation, ovalbumin, ovotransferrin.
-- source: Mannott, Bosse and Gibis, "Recent Advances in Egg White Biotechnology, Microbiology, and Quality", Comprehensive Reviews in Food Science and Food Safety 25:e70495, 2026, https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.70495 (Table 1) ; Wang et al., "The Thermodynamic and Gelation Properties of Ovalbumin and Lysozyme", Gels 11(6):470, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/gels11060470

Tilt the pan and roll the omelette out onto a warm plate. Scatter over @chives{1%tbsp}(finely chopped).

> Safety: this omelette is soft in the middle, which is below official
> advice. The US FDA says to cook eggs until both yolk and white are
> firm, and that scrambled eggs should not be runny. To follow it,
> leave the pan covered for longer until the egg is set through, or
> use pasteurised eggs. The advice matters most for children, older
> adults and pregnant women.
-- source: US Food and Drug Administration, "What You Need to Know About Egg Safety", content current as of 2024-03-05, https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/what-you-need-know-about-egg-safety

> Next time, without the recipe: beaten eggs, a moderately hot non-stick
> pan, and constant stirring. Stop while it still looks slightly
> underdone, cover it, and let its own heat finish it.
